ADDART({id:"castles",t:"Castles",c:"History",i:"🏰",w:"Castle",r:["medieurope","crusades","gothic","architecture","japan"],
p:[["Fortified homes","A castle was a fortified home for a lord or ruler, built to be defended in war."],["Stone and walls","Castles grew from earth and timber forts into great stone fortresses with towers and moats."],["Cannons changed everything","Gunpowder weapons made castle walls easier to break, and castles slowly gave way to palaces and forts."]],
l:"A castle is a fortified building that served as both the home of a lord or ruler and a stronghold for defending the surrounding land. Castles were built across Europe and the Middle East from about the 9th to the 16th century, and similar fortresses rose in Japan and elsewhere. The earliest were mounds of earth topped with wooden towers; later castles had massive stone keeps, high curtain walls, towers, moats and drawbridges. Castles were centers of government, justice and local life as well as military bases. As cannons became powerful enough to batter down walls, castles lost their military value, and many became ruins, stately homes or museums.",
s:[["Motte and bailey","Many early castles in Europe, especially after the Norman conquest of England in 1066, were of the motte-and-bailey type. A wooden tower stood on a tall earth mound, the motte, beside a fenced yard, the bailey, where people, animals and workshops were kept. They could be built in weeks using local labor and timber."],["Stone fortresses","From the late 11th century, lords began building in stone, which did not burn and was much harder to attack. A great tower, or keep, held the lord’s hall and rooms, while thick curtain walls with towers enclosed the courtyard. The tops of the walls had battlements, gaps through which archers could shoot while sheltering behind the solid parts."],["Defending a castle","Attackers tried to climb walls with ladders, batter gates, tunnel underneath or starve the defenders out in a long siege. Castles fought back with moats, drawbridges, iron grilles called portcullises, narrow arrow slits and holes for dropping stones. Concentric castles, with one ring of walls inside another, such as those built by Edward I in Wales, were especially hard to capture."],["Castles beyond Europe","Crusaders built great castles in the Middle East, such as Krak des Chevaliers in Syria. In Japan, lords built castles with stone bases and tall wooden towers, like Himeji Castle, completed in its present form in 1609. Fortified palaces and forts were also built in India, Africa and many other places."]],
k:[["Main period","About 900–1500 in Europe"],["Early type","Motte and bailey"],["Key parts","Keep, curtain wall, moat, gatehouse"],["Largest by area","Malbork Castle, Poland"],["Famous in Asia","Himeji Castle, Japan"]],
d:[["c.900s","Fortified strongholds spread in western Europe"],["1066","Normans build many motte-and-bailey castles in England"],["1100s","Great stone keeps and Crusader castles"],["1280s","Edward I builds concentric castles in Wales"],["1400s","Cannons begin to make castle walls vulnerable"],["1609","Himeji Castle in Japan reaches its present form"]],
s2:[["Life inside a castle","A castle was a busy community. Besides the lord’s family and soldiers, there were cooks, blacksmiths, carpenters, grooms, servants and priests. Most rooms were cold and smoky, lit by candles and warmed by fireplaces. The great hall was used for meals, meetings, feasts and courts of justice."],["The end of the castle age","By the 1400s, powerful cannons could smash stone walls in days. Rulers began to build low, thick star-shaped forts designed to withstand artillery, and to live in comfortable palaces without defenses. Many castles were abandoned, some were deliberately damaged after wars, and others were turned into grand homes."]],
sp:["Windsor Castle","Windsor Castle, west of London, was founded by William the Conqueror around 1070 and has been a royal residence for over 900 years, longer than any other castle in Europe. It is said to be the largest and oldest occupied castle in the world, and it is still used by the British royal family."],
g:[["Keep","The strongest tower at the heart of a castle"],["Moat","A deep ditch, often filled with water, around a castle"],["Portcullis","A heavy iron or wooden grille that drops to block a gateway"],["Siege","Surrounding a castle to force it to surrender"]],
f:["Some castle toilets, called garderobes, emptied straight down the outside wall.","Malbork Castle in Poland is the largest castle in the world by land area.","Many castles took ten years or more to build."],
fun:"The battlements on top of castle walls are also called crenellations.",
tl:"Ang kastilyo ay matibay na tirahan ng isang panginoon o pinuno na itinayo upang maipagtanggol sa digmaan. Lumaki ang mga ito mula sa mga kutang lupa at kahoy hanggang sa malalaking kutang bato na may tore at moat, ngunit nawalan ng silbi nang dumating ang kanyon."});

ADDART({id:"pirates",t:"Pirates",c:"History",i:"🏴‍☠️",w:"Piracy",r:["explor","ships","slavetrade","spice","viking"],
p:[["Robbers of the sea","Pirates attack ships or coasts to steal cargo, take hostages or demand ransom."],["A golden age","Pirates were especially active in the Caribbean between about 1650 and 1730."],["Still a problem","Piracy still happens today in some waters, though navies work to stop it."]],
l:"Piracy is robbery or violence at sea, carried out by people acting outside the authority of any government. Pirates have existed for as long as people have sailed and traded, from the Mediterranean in ancient times to the Vikings, the Barbary corsairs of North Africa, the pirates of the South China Sea and the famous Caribbean pirates of the late 1600s and early 1700s. Many popular ideas about pirates, such as buried treasure maps and walking the plank, come more from novels and films than from history. Piracy has never completely disappeared: in recent decades, pirates have attacked ships off Somalia, in the Gulf of Guinea and in parts of Southeast Asia.",
s:[["Pirates and privateers","A pirate attacked any ship for personal gain. A privateer, by contrast, was a private sailor given an official document called a letter of marque, which allowed them to attack enemy ships in wartime and share the loot with their government. The line was often thin: when wars ended, many privateers simply carried on as pirates."],["The golden age of piracy","Between about 1650 and 1730, pirates raided treasure ships and merchant vessels in the Caribbean, along the coasts of the Americas, off West Africa and in the Indian Ocean. Famous names include Blackbeard, Bartholomew Roberts, and Anne Bonny and Mary Read, two women who sailed as pirates. Navies eventually hunted most of them down."],["Pirate life","Pirate crews often voted for their captains and agreed written rules, called articles, on how to share treasure and compensate the injured. Life at sea was hard and dangerous, with disease, storms and the constant threat of capture and hanging. Flags such as the skull-and-crossbones Jolly Roger were flown to frighten victims into surrendering without a fight."],["Piracy today","Modern pirates use fast boats and weapons to board cargo ships, steal goods or hold crews for ransom. Attacks off the coast of Somalia peaked around 2009–2011, then fell sharply after international naval patrols, armed guards on ships and action on land. Piracy and armed robbery at sea remain a concern in the Gulf of Guinea and some Asian waters."]],
k:[["Golden age","About 1650–1730"],["Famous flag","The Jolly Roger"],["Legal raider","A privateer, with a letter of marque"],["Famous pirate","Blackbeard (Edward Teach)"],["Modern hotspot","Gulf of Guinea"]],
d:[["c.75 BC","Pirates capture the young Julius Caesar and hold him for ransom"],["800s–1000s","Viking raids along European coasts"],["1500s–1700s","Barbary corsairs raid from North Africa"],["c.1650–1730","The golden age of Caribbean piracy"],["1718","Blackbeard is killed off North Carolina"],["2009–11","Piracy off Somalia peaks"]],
s2:[["Zheng Yi Sao","Zheng Yi Sao, also known as Ching Shih, led one of the largest pirate fleets in history in the South China Sea in the early 1800s, commanding many hundreds of ships and tens of thousands of pirates. She enforced strict rules on her crews. In 1810, she negotiated a surrender with the Chinese government that let her keep her wealth, and she lived until 1844."],["Myths and reality","Robert Louis Stevenson’s novel Treasure Island (1883) gave the world treasure maps marked with an X, peg legs and parrots. In reality, pirates usually spent their loot quickly rather than burying it, and there is little evidence that they made captives walk the plank."]],
sp:["Blackbeard","Edward Teach, known as Blackbeard, terrorized ships in the Caribbean and off the American coast around 1716–1718. He is said to have tied slow-burning fuses into his beard to look frightening in battle. He was killed in a fierce fight with a British naval force in November 1718."],
g:[["Privateer","A private sailor licensed by a government to attack enemy ships"],["Letter of marque","An official permit to capture enemy ships"],["Corsair","A pirate or privateer, especially of the Mediterranean"],["Jolly Roger","A pirate flag, usually a skull and crossbones"]],
f:["Pirates captured Julius Caesar when he was young and released him for a ransom.","Many pirate crews voted for their captains.","The skull and crossbones was not the only pirate flag; many used their own designs."],
fun:"Some pirate crews offered payments to members injured in battle, an early form of insurance.",
tl:"Ang pirata ay umaatake sa mga barko o baybayin upang magnakaw, mangidnap o humingi ng pantubos. Kilala ang mga pirata ng Caribbean noong mga 1650 hanggang 1730, ngunit may pirata pa rin hanggang ngayon sa ilang karagatan."});

ADDART({id:"samurai",t:"The Samurai",c:"History",i:"⚔️",w:"Samurai",r:["japan","meiji","martial","calligraphy","japancountry"],
p:[["Warriors of Japan","The samurai were a warrior class that served Japan’s lords for about 700 years."],["Swords and honor","They were famous for their skill with sword and bow and for ideals of loyalty and honor."],["An end in 1870s","Japan abolished the samurai class during its rapid modernization in the 1870s."]],
l:"The samurai were the hereditary military nobility of Japan from about the 12th century until the 1870s. They began as mounted archers who served powerful families and rose to dominate Japanese government under military rulers called shoguns. Samurai were trained in archery, swordsmanship and horsemanship, but many were also educated in literature, calligraphy, poetry and the tea ceremony. During the long peace of the Tokugawa period from 1603, most samurai became officials and administrators rather than fighters. After the Meiji Restoration of 1868, the new government abolished their privileges, banned the wearing of swords and created a modern army of conscripts.",
s:[["Rise of the samurai","From the 10th century, provincial warrior families grew powerful by protecting estates and fighting rebels. Two great clans, the Taira and the Minamoto, fought a civil war that ended in 1185. Minamoto no Yoritomo then set up a military government in Kamakura and in 1192 received the title of shogun, beginning centuries of rule by warriors in the emperor’s name."],["Weapons and armor","Early samurai fought mainly as archers on horseback. Later, the curved sword called the katana became their most famous weapon, worn together with a shorter sword as a sign of samurai status. Their armor was made of small plates of lacquered iron or leather laced together with silk cords, light enough to allow movement."],["Bushido","Samurai were expected to show loyalty to their lord, courage, self-discipline and honor. These ideals were later called bushido, ‘the way of the warrior’, a term made famous by a book written in English by Nitobe Inazō in 1899. Historians note that real samurai often fell short of these ideals, and that the code was shaped and romanticized over time."],["The end of the samurai","After Commodore Perry’s American ships arrived in 1853, Japan decided to modernize quickly. The samurai class was abolished in stages, and in 1876 samurai were forbidden to wear swords in public. Some resisted: the Satsuma Rebellion of 1877, led by Saigō Takamori, was crushed by the new national army."]],
k:[["Period","About 1100s–1870s"],["Famous weapon","The katana"],["Military ruler","The shogun"],["Code of conduct","Bushido"],["Abolished","1870s, during the Meiji era"]],
d:[["1185","The Minamoto clan wins the Genpei War"],["1192","Minamoto no Yoritomo becomes shogun"],["1274, 1281","Samurai defend Japan against Mongol invasions"],["1600","Battle of Sekigahara"],["1603","Tokugawa shogunate begins a long peace"],["1876","Samurai banned from wearing swords"],["1877","Satsuma Rebellion, the last samurai uprising"]],
s2:[["The Mongol invasions","In 1274 and 1281, the Mongol Empire under Kublai Khan sent huge fleets to invade Japan. Samurai fought the invaders on the beaches, and both times storms wrecked much of the Mongol fleet. The Japanese called these storms kamikaze, ‘divine winds’."],["Women warriors","Although most samurai were men, some women of samurai families trained in martial arts, especially with a long curved spear called the naginata, to defend their homes. Tomoe Gozen, a 12th-century warrior, is celebrated in Japanese literature."]],
sp:["The 47 rōnin","A rōnin was a samurai without a master. In 1701, a lord named Asano was forced to take his own life after attacking an official. Nearly two years later, 47 of his former samurai avenged him and then were ordered to die. Their story of loyalty became one of Japan’s most famous legends."],
g:[["Shogun","A military ruler who governed Japan in the emperor’s name"],["Katana","A long, curved, single-edged samurai sword"],["Daimyo","A powerful lord who employed samurai"],["Rōnin","A samurai without a master"]],
f:["Samurai made up only a small share of Japan’s population, around 5 to 6 percent in the 1800s.","Many samurai wrote poetry and practised calligraphy.","Wearing two swords was a sign of samurai status."],
fun:"Samurai armor was so carefully made that many suits are displayed today as works of art.",
tl:"Ang samurai ay uri ng mandirigma sa Hapon na naglingkod sa mga panginoon nang mga 700 taon. Kilala sila sa husay sa espada at pana at sa katapatan at dangal, at inalis ang kanilang uri noong dekada 1870."});

ADDART({id:"floods",t:"Floods",c:"Geography",i:"🌊",w:"Flood",r:["storms","monsoon","climchange","disasterprep","wetlands"],
p:[["Too much water","A flood happens when water covers land that is normally dry."],["The most common disaster","Floods affect more people than any other natural hazard."],["Living with floods","Warnings, barriers, wetlands and good planning help reduce flood damage."]],
l:"A flood is an overflow of water onto land that is usually dry. Floods are the most common natural disaster and affect more people worldwide than any other. They can be caused by heavy or long-lasting rain, rivers bursting their banks, melting snow, storm surges driven by tropical cyclones, or the failure of dams and levees. Floods can destroy homes, crops, roads and bridges, spread disease and cause many deaths, especially flash floods that arrive with little warning. Yet floods are also natural: for thousands of years, river floods spread fertile silt across floodplains, supporting farming in places such as the Nile valley. Climate change is making heavy rainfall and coastal flooding more frequent in many regions.",
s:[["Kinds of floods","River floods happen when rivers overflow after heavy rain or snowmelt. Flash floods rise within minutes or hours, often in valleys or dry areas after intense storms. Coastal floods are caused by storm surges and high tides. Urban floods occur when rain falls faster than drains can carry it away from paved city surfaces."],["Why floods are getting worse","A warmer atmosphere holds more moisture, so heavy downpours are becoming more intense. Rising sea levels make coastal flooding more likely. Cutting down forests, draining wetlands and covering land with concrete mean that rain runs off faster instead of soaking into the ground."],["Protecting against floods","People build levees, sea walls, dams and flood barriers, and dredge rivers. Many places now also make room for water, restoring wetlands and floodplains that soak up floods naturally. Early warning systems, flood maps, raised buildings and evacuation plans save lives."],["Staying safe","Never walk, swim or drive through floodwater: just 15 centimeters of fast-moving water can knock a person over, and 60 centimeters can float a car. Floodwater may also be contaminated or hide dangers such as open drains and fallen power lines. Follow official warnings and move to higher ground."]],
k:[["Most common","The most frequent natural disaster"],["Main causes","Heavy rain, rivers, storm surges"],["Fastest kind","Flash floods"],["Natural defense","Wetlands and floodplains"],["Deadliest on record","1931 China floods"]],
d:[["c.3000 BC","Egyptian farming relies on the yearly Nile flood"],["1931","Floods in central China kill an enormous number of people"],["1953","North Sea flood strikes the Netherlands and Britain"],["1970","Aswan High Dam ends the Nile’s yearly flood"],["1997","The Netherlands completes the Delta Works"],["2022","Floods cover about a third of Pakistan"]],
s2:[["The Dutch and the sea","About a quarter of the Netherlands lies below sea level. After a disastrous North Sea flood in 1953 killed about 1,800 people, the Dutch built the Delta Works, a vast system of dams, sluices and storm-surge barriers. More recently, their ‘Room for the River’ program moved dikes back to give rivers space to flood safely."],["Pakistan, 2022","In 2022, extreme monsoon rains and melting glaciers caused floods that covered about a third of Pakistan at their peak. Over 1,700 people died and around 33 million were affected, and millions of homes, roads and crops were destroyed."]],
sp:["The Thames Barrier","London’s Thames Barrier, opened in 1982, is a row of huge steel gates across the River Thames. When a storm surge threatens, the gates rotate upwards to block the tide and protect the city. It has been closed over 200 times."],
g:[["Floodplain","Flat land beside a river that floods naturally"],["Levee","A raised bank built to keep a river from overflowing"],["Storm surge","A rise in sea level pushed ashore by a storm"],["Flash flood","A flood that rises very quickly after intense rain"]],
f:["Just 15 centimeters of fast-moving water can knock an adult off their feet.","Floods affect more people worldwide than any other natural disaster.","The ancient Egyptians measured the Nile’s flood with structures called nilometers."],
fun:"Before the Aswan High Dam, the Nile’s yearly flood left behind a layer of rich black mud that gave Egypt its ancient name, Kemet, ‘the black land’.",
tl:"Ang baha ay pag-apaw ng tubig sa lupang karaniwang tuyo. Ito ang pinakamadalas na sakuna sa mundo, at nakatutulong ang babala, harang, wetlands at maayos na pagpaplano upang mabawasan ang pinsala."});

ADDART({id:"droughts",t:"Droughts",c:"Geography",i:"🏜️",w:"Drought",r:["desert","sahel","waterscarcity","climchange","agri"],
p:[["Long dry spells","A drought is a long period with much less water than usual."],["Slow but serious","Droughts develop slowly but can ruin crops, dry up rivers and cause famine."],["Saving water","Better irrigation, water storage and planning help communities cope."]],
l:"A drought is a long period of unusually low rainfall that leads to a shortage of water. Unlike floods or storms, droughts creep up slowly and can last for months or years, so they are sometimes called a ‘creeping disaster’. They dry out soils, shrink rivers, lakes and reservoirs, kill crops and livestock, fuel wildfires and can lead to food shortages and famine, especially in poor rural areas. Droughts can strike almost anywhere, from the Sahel and the Horn of Africa to Australia, the western United States and southern Europe. Natural patterns such as El Niño play a role, and climate change is making droughts more frequent and severe in many regions.",
s:[["Types of drought","Scientists describe several types. A meteorological drought is a lack of rain. An agricultural drought is when soils become too dry for crops. A hydrological drought is when rivers, lakes and groundwater fall low. When water shortages begin to affect people’s lives and the economy, it is called a socioeconomic drought."],["Causes","Droughts happen when weather patterns keep rain-bearing systems away from a region for a long time, often linked to ocean patterns such as El Niño and La Niña. High temperatures increase evaporation, drying land faster. Overuse of rivers and groundwater for farming and cities can make the effects much worse."],["Impacts","Droughts can destroy harvests and livestock, raising food prices far beyond the affected area. They can force people to leave their homes, increase wildfires, damage forests and wildlife, and reduce hydroelectric power and river transport. In the worst cases, combined with conflict and poverty, they contribute to famine."],["Coping with drought","Communities store water in reservoirs and tanks, use drip irrigation that delivers water directly to plant roots, recycle wastewater and, in some coastal places, remove salt from seawater. Drought-resistant crops, early warnings and water-saving habits at home all help."]],
k:[["Nickname","A ‘creeping disaster’"],["Linked to","El Niño and climate change"],["Famous example","The Dust Bowl, USA, 1930s"],["Water-saving method","Drip irrigation"],["Related danger","Wildfires"]],
d:[["1876–79","Drought and famine across India and China"],["1930s","The Dust Bowl on the US Great Plains"],["1970s–80s","Severe droughts across the Sahel"],["2011","Drought contributes to famine in Somalia"],["2018","Cape Town narrowly avoids running out of water"],["2000s–","A long ‘megadrought’ in the southwestern US"]],
s2:[["The Dust Bowl","In the 1930s, years of drought struck the Great Plains of the United States, where grasslands had been plowed up for wheat. Without roots to hold it, the dry soil blew away in enormous dust storms called ‘black blizzards’. Hundreds of thousands of people left their farms. The disaster led to new soil conservation methods."],["Cape Town’s Day Zero","In 2018, after three dry years, Cape Town in South Africa warned of a ‘Day Zero’ when its taps would be turned off. Residents were limited to 50 liters of water a day each. By saving water dramatically, the city avoided Day Zero until the rains returned."]],
sp:["Drip irrigation","Drip irrigation delivers small amounts of water through tubes directly to the roots of each plant, wasting far less than flooding fields or spraying. Developed in Israel in the 1960s, it is now used by farmers around the world to grow more food with less water."],
g:[["Drought","A long period of unusually low rainfall and water"],["Reservoir","A lake, often artificial, used to store water"],["Groundwater","Water stored underground in soil and rock"],["Desalination","Removing salt from seawater to make fresh water"]],
f:["During the Dust Bowl, dust from the Great Plains fell on ships in the Atlantic Ocean.","Cape Town’s residents cut their water use by about half in 2018.","Some droughts have lasted for decades."],
fun:"Tree rings can reveal droughts from hundreds of years ago, because trees grow thinner rings in dry years.",
tl:"Ang tagtuyot ay mahabang panahon na kulang na kulang ang ulan at tubig. Mabagal itong dumating ngunit maaaring sumira ng ani, magpatuyo ng ilog at magdulot ng gutom, kaya mahalaga ang pagtitipid at maayos na patubig."});

ADDART({id:"snow",t:"Snow",c:"Geography",i:"❄️",w:"Snow",r:["storms","glaciers","iceage","weatherforecast","climchange"],
p:[["Frozen crystals","Snow is made of tiny ice crystals that form in clouds and fall to the ground."],["Six-sided flakes","Most snowflakes have six sides because of the way water molecules join together."],["Water for the world","Melting snow supplies drinking water and farming for billions of people."]],
l:"Snow is precipitation made of ice crystals that form in clouds when water vapor freezes around tiny particles of dust or pollen. As the crystals fall, they grow and stick together into snowflakes. Snow falls in cold regions and on high mountains, even near the equator, as on Kilimanjaro. A fresh layer of snow is mostly trapped air, so it insulates the ground and reflects most sunlight back into space, helping keep the planet cool. Snow is important for water supplies: melting mountain snowpack feeds rivers used for drinking and farming by billions of people. Heavy snowfall can also bring blizzards and avalanches that disrupt travel and endanger lives.",
s:[["How snowflakes form","Inside cold clouds, water vapor freezes onto specks of dust, forming tiny ice crystals. Because of the way water molecules bond, the crystals grow in six-sided patterns. Temperature and humidity decide their shape: thin plates, needles, columns or the familiar branching stars. Each flake takes a slightly different path through the cloud, so no two are exactly alike."],["Snow and climate","Fresh snow reflects up to about 90 percent of sunlight, far more than bare ground or sea. When snow and ice melt, darker land and water absorb more heat, which causes more warming. Scientists watch snow cover closely because it is shrinking in many places as the climate warms."],["Blizzards and avalanches","A blizzard is a storm with strong winds and blowing snow that makes it almost impossible to see. An avalanche is a mass of snow sliding rapidly down a mountain slope, often triggered when a weak layer within the snowpack gives way. Forecasters warn of avalanche danger, and rescuers use beacons and trained dogs to find people buried in snow."],["Living with snow","People in snowy regions have long adapted, building steep roofs, using skis, sleds and snowshoes, and in the Arctic, building snow shelters. Snow brings winter sports and tourism, but also challenges for roads, power lines and farming."]],
k:[["Made of","Ice crystals"],["Flake shape","Usually six-sided"],["Sunlight reflected","Up to about 90 percent"],["Danger","Blizzards and avalanches"],["Snow on the equator","Kilimanjaro and Andes peaks"]],
d:[["1611","Johannes Kepler writes about why snowflakes have six sides"],["1885","Wilson Bentley takes the first photographs of single snowflakes"],["1927","Record snow depth of 11.8 m on Mount Ibuki, Japan"],["1930s","Ukichiro Nakaya grows the first artificial snowflakes"],["1960s","Satellites begin mapping snow cover from space"]],
s2:[["The Snowflake Man","Wilson Bentley, a farmer in Vermont, USA, was the first person to photograph individual snowflakes, in 1885, using a camera attached to a microscope. Over his lifetime he photographed more than 5,000 snowflakes and concluded that no two were alike."],["Snow in the tropics","Snow can fall even near the equator on very high mountains, where the air is cold. Mount Kilimanjaro in Tanzania and peaks in the Andes and in New Guinea have permanent snow or ice, though these tropical glaciers are shrinking fast."]],
sp:["Snow country","Some of the snowiest inhabited places on Earth are on the western coast of Japan, where cold winds from Siberia pick up moisture over the Sea of Japan and dump it as snow. Several meters of snow can fall in a winter, and some roads pass between walls of snow taller than a bus."],
g:[["Snowpack","The layers of snow that build up on the ground over winter"],["Blizzard","A snowstorm with strong winds and very poor visibility"],["Avalanche","A large mass of snow sliding quickly down a slope"],["Albedo","How much sunlight a surface reflects"]],
f:["Fresh snow can be over 90 percent air.","Snow looks white because its ice crystals scatter all colors of light.","Wilson Bentley photographed more than 5,000 snowflakes."],
fun:"Falling snow absorbs sound, which is why a snowy landscape seems so quiet.",
tl:"Ang niyebe ay maliliit na kristal ng yelo na nabubuo sa ulap at bumabagsak sa lupa. Karaniwang anim ang gilid ng mga snowflake, at ang natutunaw na niyebe sa kabundukan ay pinagkukunan ng tubig ng bilyun-bilyong tao."});

ADDART({id:"yoga",t:"Yoga and Meditation",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🧘",w:"Yoga",r:["hindu","buddh","stress","exercise","mental"],
p:[["An ancient practice","Yoga began in ancient India as a spiritual discipline for body and mind."],["Training attention","Meditation means training the mind to focus, often on the breath."],["Popular worldwide","Today, millions practise yoga and meditation for health and wellbeing."]],
l:"Yoga is a group of physical, mental and spiritual practices that began in ancient India. In its traditional form, it aimed at stilling the mind and reaching spiritual liberation, through ethics, breathing exercises, meditation and physical postures. Meditation, a central part of yoga, is also practised in Buddhism, Jainism, Daoism, Christianity, Islam and other traditions, and involves training attention, often by focusing on the breath, a word or a sensation. In the 20th century, yoga focused on postures spread around the world, and secular forms of meditation such as mindfulness became popular. Research suggests these practices can help reduce stress and improve flexibility and wellbeing, though they are not a substitute for medical treatment.",
s:[["Origins","Ideas of yoga appear in ancient Indian texts such as the Upanishads and the Bhagavad Gita. The Yoga Sutras of Patanjali, compiled roughly 2,000 years ago, describe yoga as the stilling of the movements of the mind and set out an eight-limbed path. Yoga is part of several Indian religious and philosophical traditions."],["The eight limbs","Patanjali’s path includes ethical rules (yama and niyama), postures (asana), breath control (pranayama), withdrawal of the senses (pratyahara), concentration (dharana), meditation (dhyana) and absorption (samadhi). Postures were only one part of this path."],["Modern yoga","In the early 20th century, teachers in India such as Tirumalai Krishnamacharya developed flowing sequences of postures. Their students spread these styles worldwide. The United Nations declared 21 June the International Day of Yoga in 2014, and UNESCO added yoga to its list of intangible cultural heritage in 2016."],["Meditation and mindfulness","Meditation can involve focusing on the breath, repeating a word, observing thoughts without judging them, or cultivating kindness. Mindfulness, drawn from Buddhist practice, was adapted for health care in 1979 by Jon Kabat-Zinn. Studies suggest meditation may reduce stress and anxiety for many people, though results vary."]],
k:[["Origin","Ancient India"],["Classic text","Yoga Sutras of Patanjali"],["Yoga Day","21 June, since 2015"],["UNESCO heritage","2016"],["Popular secular form","Mindfulness"]],
d:[["c.500 BC","Early ideas of yoga and meditation in Indian texts"],["c.200 BC–AD 400","Patanjali’s Yoga Sutras compiled"],["1893","Swami Vivekananda introduces yoga ideas in Chicago"],["1930s","Krishnamacharya develops modern posture sequences"],["1979","Mindfulness-based stress reduction begins"],["2015","First International Day of Yoga"]],
s2:[["Yoga goes global","In 1893, Swami Vivekananda spoke at the World’s Parliament of Religions in Chicago, introducing Indian philosophy and yoga to many Westerners. In the following decades, Indian teachers founded schools abroad, and by the late 20th century yoga classes had spread to gyms, schools and hospitals worldwide."],["Many traditions of meditation","Buddhist monks practise meditation to develop insight and compassion. Christian monks practise contemplative prayer, Sufi Muslims repeat the names of God, and Daoists cultivate stillness. Although their beliefs differ, these traditions share the idea of training the mind through quiet, focused attention."]],
sp:["Breathing exercises","Many yoga and meditation practices begin with slow, deep breathing. Breathing out slowly can activate the body’s ‘rest and digest’ system, slowing the heart rate and calming the mind. Simple breathing exercises are now taught in schools, workplaces and sports teams."],
g:[["Asana","A yoga posture"],["Pranayama","Yoga breathing exercises"],["Meditation","Training the mind to focus attention"],["Mindfulness","Paying calm attention to the present moment"]],
f:["The word yoga comes from a Sanskrit root meaning ‘to join’ or ‘to yoke’.","International Day of Yoga is held on 21 June.","Meditation is practised in many religions and also without religion."],
fun:"The popular ‘lotus position’ for meditation is named after the lotus flower, a symbol of purity in Indian traditions.",
tl:"Ang yoga ay sinaunang gawain mula sa India para sa katawan at isipan, at ang meditasyon ay pagsasanay ng isip na magtuon, kadalasan sa paghinga. Milyun-milyong tao ngayon ang gumagawa nito para sa kalusugan."});

ADDART({id:"writing",t:"The History of Writing",c:"Languages",i:"✍️",w:"History of writing",r:["alphabet","mesop","egypt","paper","print"],
p:[["A world-changing invention","Writing lets people record language so it can last and travel."],["Invented several times","Writing was invented independently in Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica."],["Many systems","Scripts can use signs for words, syllables or single sounds."]],
l:"Writing is a system of visible signs used to record spoken language. It was one of the most important inventions in history, allowing people to keep records, write laws, send messages, preserve stories and knowledge, and communicate across distance and time. Writing appears to have been invented independently at least three or four times: in Mesopotamia and Egypt around 3200 BC, in China by about 1200 BC, and in Mesoamerica by the first millennium BC. The first writing was used mainly for counting goods and keeping accounts. Over time, scripts spread, changed and multiplied, and the alphabet, developed around 1800 BC, became the ancestor of many of the scripts used today.",
s:[["From tokens to tablets","In Mesopotamia, people first used small clay tokens to count goods. By around 3200 BC, scribes in the city of Uruk were pressing pictures and numbers into clay tablets to record grain, animals and workers. These signs developed into cuneiform, wedge-shaped marks made with a reed, used to write many languages for over 3,000 years."],["Different kinds of scripts","Some scripts use signs for whole words or meanings, called logograms, as in Chinese characters. Others use signs for syllables, as in Japanese kana. Alphabets use letters for individual sounds. Abjads, such as Arabic and Hebrew, mainly write consonants, and abugidas, such as Devanagari used for Hindi, combine consonants with marks for vowels."],["The alphabet","Around 1800 BC, workers speaking a Semitic language in Egypt and Sinai created signs for single consonant sounds, based on Egyptian hieroglyphs. This idea developed into the Phoenician alphabet, which the Greeks adapted around 800 BC by adding vowels. From Greek came the Latin and Cyrillic alphabets, now used by billions of people."],["Decipherment","Many ancient scripts were forgotten for centuries. In 1822, Jean-François Champollion deciphered Egyptian hieroglyphs using the Rosetta Stone, which carried the same text in hieroglyphs, a later Egyptian script and Greek. Maya writing was largely deciphered in the late 20th century, but the Indus script of ancient South Asia remains unread."]],
k:[["First writing","Cuneiform, about 3200 BC"],["Invented independently","At least three or four times"],["Oldest Chinese writing","Oracle bones, about 1200 BC"],["First alphabet","About 1800 BC"],["Still undeciphered","The Indus script"]],
d:[["c.3200 BC","Cuneiform in Mesopotamia; hieroglyphs in Egypt"],["c.1800 BC","The first alphabet"],["c.1200 BC","Chinese writing on oracle bones"],["c.800 BC","Greeks add vowels to the alphabet"],["c.300 BC","Maya writing develops"],["1822","Champollion deciphers hieroglyphs"]],
s2:[["Oracle bones","The oldest known Chinese writing is found on oracle bones from about 1200 BC: turtle shells and animal bones used by kings of the Shang dynasty to ask questions of their ancestors. Priests carved questions, heated the bones until they cracked and read the answers in the cracks. Many modern Chinese characters can be traced back to these inscriptions."],["Writing materials","People have written on clay, stone, bone, bamboo, palm leaves, papyrus, parchment made from animal skin, and paper, invented in China around the 2nd century BC and improved by Cai Lun in about AD 105. The materials shaped the scripts: wedges suited clay, while brushes suited Chinese characters on silk and paper."]],
sp:["The Rosetta Stone","The Rosetta Stone was found in 1799 near the town of Rashid (Rosetta) in Egypt. It records a decree from 196 BC in three scripts. Because scholars could read the Greek, it became the key to deciphering Egyptian hieroglyphs. It is now in the British Museum."],
g:[["Script","A system of signs for writing a language"],["Cuneiform","Ancient wedge-shaped writing pressed into clay"],["Hieroglyphs","The picture-based writing of ancient Egypt"],["Logogram","A written sign that stands for a whole word"]],
f:["Some of the oldest writing in the world is about beer and grain rations.","Cuneiform was used for over 3,000 years.","The Indus script of ancient South Asia has still not been deciphered."],
fun:"The word ‘alphabet’ comes from alpha and beta, the first two letters of the Greek alphabet.",
tl:"Ang pagsulat ay sistema ng mga tanda na nagtatala ng wika. Naimbento ito nang hiwalay sa Mesopotamia, Ehipto, Tsina at Mesoamerica, at ang alpabeto ang naging ninuno ng maraming sulat na ginagamit ngayon."});

ADDART({id:"london",t:"London",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🎡",w:"London",r:["ukcountry","rome","indrev","megacities","tourism"],
p:[["Capital on the Thames","London is the capital of England and the United Kingdom, on the River Thames."],["Two thousand years old","It was founded by the Romans almost 2,000 years ago."],["A world city","It is a global center of finance, culture and education, home to people from all over the world."]],
l:"London is the capital and largest city of England and the United Kingdom, with about 8.9 million people in Greater London. It stands on the River Thames in south-east England. Founded by the Romans as Londinium around AD 47, it grew into the center of a global empire and is today one of the world’s leading centers of finance, business, culture, education and tourism. Its landmarks include the Tower of London, Westminster Abbey, the Houses of Parliament and the clock tower known as Big Ben, Buckingham Palace, St Paul’s Cathedral and the British Museum. London is one of the most diverse cities on Earth, where more than 300 languages are spoken.",
s:[["Roman to medieval London","The Romans built a bridge across the Thames and a walled town called Londinium, which was burned by Queen Boudica’s rebels in AD 60 or 61 and then rebuilt. After the Romans left, the city declined and later revived. William the Conqueror was crowned at Westminster in 1066 and began building the Tower of London."],["Fire, plague and growth","In 1665 a plague killed about a fifth of the population, and in 1666 the Great Fire destroyed much of the old city. It was rebuilt in stone and brick, with St Paul’s Cathedral designed by Christopher Wren. By 1900, London was the largest city in the world and the heart of the British Empire."],["War and renewal","During the Blitz of 1940–41, German bombing killed tens of thousands of Londoners and destroyed large parts of the city. After the war, London was rebuilt and welcomed immigrants from the Caribbean, South Asia, Africa and elsewhere, who reshaped its culture. Its old docks became a new business district."],["Getting around","London opened the world’s first underground railway in 1863. Today the Underground, or ‘Tube’, has 11 lines and over 270 stations. Red double-decker buses and black cabs are famous symbols of the city, and the Elizabeth line opened in 2022."]],
k:[["Country","United Kingdom"],["Population","About 8.9 million (Greater London)"],["River","Thames"],["Founded","About AD 47, as Londinium"],["Olympics","1908, 1948 and 2012"]],
d:[["c.AD 47","Romans found Londinium"],["1066","William the Conqueror crowned at Westminster"],["1666","The Great Fire of London"],["1863","The world’s first underground railway opens"],["1940–41","The Blitz"],["2012","London hosts the Olympic Games for the third time"]],
s2:[["The Great Fire","The Great Fire of London began in a bakery on Pudding Lane on 2 September 1666 and burned for four days, destroying over 13,000 houses and 87 churches. The recorded death toll was small, though it may have been higher. A tall column called the Monument stands near where the fire began."],["The Prime Meridian","At the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, a line marks the Prime Meridian, 0 degrees longitude, agreed in 1884 as the starting point for measuring longitude and time zones around the world. Visitors can stand with one foot in each hemisphere."]],
sp:["Big Ben","Big Ben is the nickname of the great bell in the clock tower of the Houses of Parliament, though it is often used for the tower itself, officially renamed the Elizabeth Tower in 2012. The bell first rang in 1859 and weighs about 13.7 tonnes."],
g:[["Thames","The river that flows through London"],["Tube","The nickname of London’s underground railway"],["The City","London’s historic center and financial district"],["Meridian","A line of longitude"]],
f:["London was the first city to host the Summer Olympics three times.","The London Underground opened in 1863.","More than 300 languages are spoken in London."],
fun:"The Tower of London has kept ravens for centuries; legend says the kingdom will fall if they leave.",
tl:"Ang London ang kabisera ng Inglatera at ng United Kingdom, sa tabi ng Ilog Thames. Itinatag ito ng mga Romano halos 2,000 taon na ang nakararaan at isa na ngayong pandaigdigang sentro ng pananalapi at kultura."});

ADDART({id:"beijing",t:"Beijing",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏯",w:"Beijing",r:["chinacountry","china","modernchina","greatwall","megacities"],
p:[["China’s capital","Beijing is the capital of China and its political and cultural center."],["Imperial city","For centuries it was the home of China’s emperors in the Forbidden City."],["A modern megacity","Today over 21 million people live there, among ancient sites and modern towers."]],
l:"Beijing is the capital of the People’s Republic of China, with a population of about 21.9 million. Located in northern China, it has been the country’s capital for most of the past 800 years, under the Yuan, Ming and Qing dynasties and since 1949. At its heart lies the Forbidden City, the vast palace of the emperors, beside Tiananmen Square. Nearby are the Temple of Heaven, the Summer Palace and sections of the Great Wall. Beijing is China’s center of government, culture and education, home to leading universities, and a major hub of technology. In 2022 it became the first city to have hosted both the Summer and Winter Olympic Games.",
s:[["An imperial capital","Kublai Khan, the Mongol ruler, built a capital called Dadu on the site in the 1260s. The Ming dynasty moved its capital there in the early 1400s and named it Beijing, ‘northern capital’. The Ming and Qing emperors ruled from the Forbidden City for nearly 500 years, until the last emperor gave up the throne in 1912."],["The Forbidden City","Built between 1406 and 1420, the Forbidden City is the largest surviving palace complex in the world, with around 980 buildings. Ordinary people were not allowed to enter, hence its name. Since 1925, it has been the Palace Museum, holding over a million works of art."],["Hutongs and courtyard homes","Old Beijing was a maze of narrow lanes called hutongs, lined with traditional courtyard houses called siheyuan. Many were demolished as the city modernized, but some neighborhoods have been preserved and restored, and people still live in them."],["Modern Beijing","Since the 1980s, Beijing has grown rapidly, with ring roads, one of the world’s largest subway systems and new business districts. Air pollution was once severe but has improved significantly since the 2010s after measures to cut coal burning and traffic emissions."]],
k:[["Country","China"],["Population","About 21.9 million (2020)"],["Meaning of name","Northern capital"],["Famous palace","The Forbidden City"],["Olympics","Summer 2008, Winter 2022"]],
d:[["1260s","Kublai Khan builds his capital, Dadu"],["1420","The Forbidden City is completed"],["1912","The last emperor gives up the throne"],["1949","Becomes capital of the People’s Republic of China"],["2008","Hosts the Summer Olympics"],["2022","Hosts the Winter Olympics"]],
s2:[["The Temple of Heaven","At the Temple of Heaven, built in the early 1400s, Ming and Qing emperors performed ceremonies to pray for good harvests. Its round, triple-roofed Hall of Prayer for Good Harvests is built of wood without nails. Today the surrounding park is popular with residents practising tai chi, singing and playing games."],["Peking duck","Beijing’s most famous dish, Peking duck, has been served since imperial times. The duck is roasted until its skin is crisp, then carved and wrapped in thin pancakes with spring onions, cucumber and sweet bean sauce."]],
sp:["Tiananmen Square","Tiananmen Square, one of the largest city squares in the world, lies in front of the Gate of Heavenly Peace, where Mao Zedong proclaimed the People’s Republic in 1949. It has been the site of major celebrations and protests, including the pro-democracy demonstrations of 1989 that were ended by a military crackdown."],
g:[["Hutong","A narrow lane in old Beijing"],["Siheyuan","A traditional courtyard house"],["Dynasty","A line of rulers from the same family"],["Forbidden City","The former palace of China’s emperors"]],
f:["Beijing means ‘northern capital’.","The Forbidden City has around 980 buildings.","Beijing is the only city to have hosted both the Summer and Winter Olympics."],
fun:"In older English books, Beijing is spelled Peking, a name still used for Peking duck and Peking opera.",
tl:"Ang Beijing ang kabisera ng Tsina at sentro ng pamahalaan at kultura nito. Dito nakatira ang mga emperador sa Forbidden City, at ngayon ay mahigit 21 milyong tao ang naninirahan dito."});

ADDART({id:"mumbai",t:"Mumbai",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌆",w:"Mumbai",r:["indiacountry","indus","bollywood","megacities","trade"],
p:[["India’s biggest city","Mumbai is India’s most populous city and its financial capital."],["Seven islands","It was built on seven islands that were joined together by land reclamation."],["Home of Bollywood","It is the center of India’s Hindi film industry."]],
l:"Mumbai, formerly known as Bombay, is the capital of the Indian state of Maharashtra and the largest city in India, with over 12 million people in the city and more than 20 million in its metropolitan region. It lies on the west coast, on a peninsula facing the Arabian Sea. Mumbai is India’s financial and commercial capital, home to the country’s main stock exchanges, the Reserve Bank of India, a huge port and the Hindi film industry known as Bollywood. It is a city of great contrasts, with luxury towers and colonial buildings beside some of Asia’s largest informal settlements. Its landmarks include the Gateway of India and Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus.",
s:[["From islands to city","The area was once seven islands, home to Koli fishing communities. Portugal took control in the 1530s and gave the islands to England in 1661 as part of the dowry of Catherine of Braganza on her marriage to King Charles II. The English East India Company developed the port, and over the 18th and 19th centuries the islands were joined by land reclamation."],["A colonial port","In the 19th century, Bombay grew rich on the cotton trade and became one of the British Empire’s greatest ports, with grand Victorian Gothic buildings. Its railway station, now Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus, completed in 1888, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The city was also a center of India’s independence movement."],["Bollywood","Mumbai is the heart of the Hindi film industry, which produces hundreds of films each year, famous for song-and-dance numbers and enjoyed across South Asia and the world. The name Bollywood combines Bombay and Hollywood."],["A city of contrasts","Millions of people commute daily on Mumbai’s crowded suburban trains. Dharavi, one of Asia’s largest informal settlements, is also a busy hub of small industries such as recycling, pottery and leatherwork. The city faces challenges of housing, traffic and monsoon flooding."]],
k:[["Country","India"],["State","Maharashtra"],["Population","Over 12 million (city); over 20 million (region)"],["Former name","Bombay (renamed 1995)"],["Famous landmark","Gateway of India"]],
d:[["1534","Portugal takes control of the islands"],["1661","The islands are given to England"],["1668","The East India Company takes over"],["1888","The Victoria Terminus railway station is completed"],["1924","The Gateway of India is opened"],["1995","The city is officially renamed Mumbai"]],
s2:[["The dabbawalas","Every day, Mumbai’s dabbawalas deliver around 200,000 home-cooked lunches in metal tiffin boxes to offices across the city, using bicycles, trains and handcarts, and return the empty boxes in the afternoon. They use a system of colored codes, and their deliveries are famously reliable."],["The monsoon","Mumbai receives most of its yearly rain during the monsoon from June to September, and in July 2005 more than 900 millimeters fell in a single day, flooding large parts of the city. The monsoon is also celebrated, with people gathering on the seafront to watch the waves."]],
sp:["The Gateway of India","The Gateway of India is a large stone arch on the waterfront, built to commemorate the visit of King George V and Queen Mary in 1911 and completed in 1924. In 1948, the last British troops left India through it, making it a symbol of the end of colonial rule."],
g:[["Reclamation","Creating new land from the sea or marshes"],["Dabbawala","A lunchbox delivery worker in Mumbai"],["Bollywood","The Hindi film industry based in Mumbai"],["Monsoon","A seasonal wind that brings heavy rain"]],
f:["Mumbai was built on seven islands.","Its name comes from Mumba Devi, a local Hindu goddess.","Mumbai’s suburban trains carry millions of passengers every day."],
fun:"Mumbai’s Marine Drive is nicknamed the ‘Queen’s Necklace’ because its streetlights curve like a string of pearls at night.",
tl:"Ang Mumbai, dating Bombay, ang pinakamalaking lungsod sa India at sentro ng pananalapi nito. Itinayo ito sa pitong pulo na pinagdugtong, at dito nakabase ang industriya ng pelikulang Bollywood."});

ADDART({id:"nairobi",t:"Nairobi",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦒",w:"Nairobi",r:["kenyacountry","rift","africacolon","megacities","conservation"],
p:[["Kenya’s capital","Nairobi is the capital and largest city of Kenya, in East Africa."],["Wildlife next door","It has a national park with lions and giraffes right next to the city."],["A hub for Africa","It is a center of business, technology and international organizations."]],
l:"Nairobi is the capital and largest city of Kenya, with about 4.4 million people at the 2019 census. It lies in the highlands of southern Kenya at about 1,795 meters above sea level, which gives it a mild climate. The city began in 1899 as a depot on the railway being built from Mombasa to Lake Victoria, and grew to become the capital of British East Africa and, from 1963, of independent Kenya. Today it is one of Africa’s leading business and diplomatic hubs, home to the global headquarters of the UN Environment Programme and UN-Habitat. Nairobi National Park, just south of the city center, is the only national park in the world located within a capital city.",
s:[["A railway town","The name comes from the Maasai phrase Enkare Nairobi, meaning ‘cool water’, after a stream in the area. In 1899, engineers building the Uganda Railway set up a supply depot there, and a town quickly grew. In 1905 it replaced Mombasa as the capital of the British East Africa Protectorate."],["Independence and growth","Nairobi was a center of the struggle for independence, which Kenya won on 12 December 1963. The city has grown rapidly since then, attracting people from across Kenya and the region. Its fast growth has brought modern towers and businesses, but also crowded informal settlements such as Kibera, one of Africa’s largest."],["Silicon Savannah","Nairobi is known as a hub of technology and innovation, nicknamed the ‘Silicon Savannah’. Kenya pioneered mobile money with M-Pesa, launched in 2007, which lets people send and receive money by text message and is used by most Kenyan adults."],["Nature in the city","Nairobi National Park, opened in 1946, is home to lions, giraffes, zebras, buffaloes and rhinos, with the city skyline visible behind them. Nearby, a famous orphanage cares for baby elephants and rhinos rescued from the wild."]],
k:[["Country","Kenya"],["Population","About 4.4 million (2019)"],["Altitude","About 1,795 m"],["Founded","1899, as a railway depot"],["Name means","‘Cool water’ (Maasai)"]],
d:[["1899","A railway depot is set up at Nairobi"],["1905","Becomes capital of British East Africa"],["1946","Nairobi National Park is created"],["1963","Capital of independent Kenya"],["1972","UN Environment Programme headquarters established"],["2007","M-Pesa mobile money launched in Kenya"]],
s2:[["The ivory burning","In 1989 and again in 2016, Kenya burned huge piles of confiscated elephant tusks in Nairobi National Park to show its determination to stop the illegal ivory trade. The 2016 fire destroyed about 105 tonnes of ivory, one of the largest ivory burns ever."],["Wangari Maathai and Uhuru Park","In 1989, environmentalist Wangari Maathai led protests that stopped the government from building a huge skyscraper in Uhuru Park, one of Nairobi’s few green spaces. She was beaten and arrested, but the plan was dropped. She later won the Nobel Peace Prize."]],
sp:["Matatus","Nairobi’s privately run minibuses, called matatus, carry millions of commuters. Many are painted with colorful art showing musicians, sports stars and cartoon characters, and play loud music to attract passengers."],
g:[["Depot","A place where supplies are stored"],["Matatu","A minibus used as public transport in Kenya"],["Mobile money","Paying and sending money using a mobile phone"],["Savannah","Tropical grassland with scattered trees"]],
f:["Nairobi is the only capital city with a national park inside its boundaries.","Its name means ‘cool water’ in the Maasai language.","Nairobi hosts the global headquarters of the UN Environment Programme."],
fun:"Giraffes can be seen in Nairobi National Park with skyscrapers in the background.",
tl:"Ang Nairobi ang kabisera at pinakamalaking lungsod ng Kenya. May pambansang liwasan ito na may mga leon at giraffe sa tabi mismo ng lungsod, at sentro ito ng negosyo at teknolohiya sa Africa."});

ADDART({id:"petra",t:"Petra",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏜️",w:"Petra",r:["jordancountry","rome","silk","architecture","tourism"],
p:[["Carved from rock","Petra is an ancient city with buildings carved directly into rose-red sandstone cliffs."],["A trading capital","It was the capital of the Nabataeans, who grew rich on the incense trade."],["A world wonder","It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the New Seven Wonders of the World."]],
l:"Petra is an ancient city in southern Jordan, famous for its tombs and temples carved into rose-red sandstone cliffs. It was the capital of the Nabataeans, an Arab people who grew wealthy controlling caravan routes that carried incense, spices and other goods between Arabia, Egypt and the Mediterranean. The city flourished from around the 1st century BC to the 1st century AD, with a population of perhaps 20,000 or more. It was annexed by the Roman Empire in AD 106 and later declined after trade routes shifted and earthquakes struck. Largely forgotten by the outside world, it was described by a European traveler in 1812. Today it is Jordan’s most visited attraction.",
s:[["The Nabataeans","The Nabataeans were originally nomads who settled in the region and became expert traders and engineers. They built dams, cisterns and channels to collect and store rainwater, allowing a city to thrive in the desert. Their writing developed into an early form of the Arabic script."],["Entering through the Siq","Visitors reach Petra through the Siq, a narrow gorge about 1.2 kilometers long with walls up to about 80 meters high. At its end, the Treasury, or Al-Khazneh, suddenly appears, its elaborate facade carved into the cliff. Despite its name, it was probably a royal tomb."],["The city","Petra has hundreds of rock-cut tombs, a theater that could seat several thousand people, temples, a colonnaded street and the huge Monastery, or Ad Deir, reached by climbing about 800 steps. Most ordinary houses were built of stone and have not survived."],["Decline and rediscovery","After the Romans took over, trade moved to other routes, and a major earthquake in AD 363 damaged the city. By the Middle Ages, only local Bedouin knew it. In 1812, the Swiss explorer Johann Ludwig Burckhardt visited it in disguise and described it to Europeans. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1985."]],
k:[["Country","Jordan"],["Built by","The Nabataeans"],["Famous building","The Treasury (Al-Khazneh)"],["Entrance","The Siq, a narrow gorge"],["UNESCO site","Since 1985"]],
d:[["c.300s BC","Nabataeans settle in the area"],["c.100 BC–AD 100","Petra at its height"],["AD 106","Annexed by the Roman Empire"],["AD 363","Damaged by a major earthquake"],["1812","Burckhardt describes it to Europe"],["2007","Named one of the New Seven Wonders"]],
s2:[["Water in the desert","Petra receives very little rain, but the Nabataeans built a clever system of channels, pipes, dams and cisterns to capture flash floods and store water. Some of their channels can still be seen cut into the walls of the Siq. This engineering allowed gardens and pools in the heart of the city."],["The Rose City","Petra is often called the ‘Rose City’ because of the pink and red color of its stone. The sandstone has bands of red, orange, yellow and white, which change color with the light throughout the day."]],
sp:["The Treasury","The Treasury’s facade is about 40 meters tall, carved from the top down into the cliff face. Local legend said treasure was hidden in the urn at its top, and bullet marks show where people shot at it hoping to release the riches. It was most likely the tomb of a Nabataean king."],
g:[["Nabataeans","An ancient Arab people who built Petra"],["Facade","The front face of a building"],["Cistern","A tank for storing rainwater"],["Caravan","A group of traders traveling together with animals"]],
f:["Petra’s Monastery is reached by about 800 rock-cut steps.","The Siq is about 1.2 kilometers long.","Petra was chosen as one of the New Seven Wonders of the World in 2007."],
fun:"Petra is often called the ‘Rose City’ because of the color of its stone.",
tl:"Ang Petra ay sinaunang lungsod sa Jordan na may mga gusaling inukit sa kulay-rosas na bato. Ito ang kabisera ng mga Nabateano na yumaman sa kalakalan, at isa na ito ngayon sa New Seven Wonders of the World."});

ADDART({id:"colosseum",t:"The Colosseum",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏟️",w:"Colosseum",r:["rome","italycountry","architecture","greece","tourism"],
p:[["Rome’s great arena","The Colosseum is a huge ancient amphitheater in the center of Rome."],["Games and shows","Tens of thousands watched gladiator fights and animal hunts there."],["A symbol of Rome","Though damaged, it is one of the most famous buildings in the world."]],
l:"The Colosseum, originally called the Flavian Amphitheater, is a giant oval arena in the center of Rome, Italy. Begun by Emperor Vespasian around AD 70–72 and opened by his son Titus in AD 80, it was the largest amphitheater ever built in the Roman world, able to hold an estimated 50,000 or more spectators. For centuries it hosted gladiator combats, wild animal hunts, executions and public spectacles. After the fall of the Western Roman Empire, it was damaged by earthquakes and stripped for building stone, but much of it still stands. Today it is one of the most visited monuments in the world and a symbol of ancient Rome.",
s:[["Building the Colosseum","The Colosseum was built of travertine stone, volcanic tuff, brick and Roman concrete, probably using thousands of laborers, many of them enslaved. It is about 189 meters long, 156 meters wide and about 48 meters high. Its outer wall had four levels, with arches framed by columns in different classical styles."],["Inside the arena","Spectators were seated by social rank, with senators closest to the arena and women and the poor at the top. Eighty entrances allowed crowds to enter and leave quickly. A huge canvas awning, the velarium, could be stretched over the seats to give shade. Beneath the wooden arena floor lay the hypogeum, a network of tunnels, cages and lifts."],["The games","Gladiators, many of them enslaved people or prisoners trained in special schools, fought each other with different weapons. Wild animals such as lions, bears and elephants were brought from across the empire for hunts. The opening games of AD 80 reportedly lasted 100 days. Gladiator fights ended in the 5th century."],["After Rome","Earthquakes, especially one in 1349, brought down parts of the outer wall, and for centuries its stone was taken to build churches and palaces. The Catholic Church later declared it a sacred site in memory of early Christians believed to have died in Rome. Restoration work continues today."]],
k:[["City","Rome, Italy"],["Opened","AD 80"],["Spectators","About 50,000 or more"],["Size","About 189 × 156 m, 48 m high"],["Original name","Flavian Amphitheater"]],
d:[["c.AD 70–72","Emperor Vespasian begins construction"],["AD 80","Opened by Titus with 100 days of games"],["AD 404","Last recorded gladiator fights (approximate)"],["1349","An earthquake damages the outer wall"],["1980","Historic center of Rome becomes a UNESCO site"],["2007","Named one of the New Seven Wonders"]],
s2:[["The hypogeum","Beneath the arena floor was a two-level underground maze of corridors and rooms where gladiators and animals waited. Workers used rope-and-pulley lifts and trapdoors to make animals and scenery appear suddenly in the arena, astonishing the crowd."],["A name from a statue","The name Colosseum probably comes from a colossal bronze statue of Emperor Nero that stood nearby. In the Middle Ages, people began calling the amphitheater by the statue’s name."]],
sp:["Gladiators","Gladiators came in many types, each with different armor and weapons, such as the heavily armed murmillo or the net-fighter called the retiarius, who carried a trident. Successful gladiators could become celebrities, and some won their freedom."],
g:[["Amphitheater","An oval or round open-air arena surrounded by seats"],["Gladiator","A fighter who entertained Roman crowds in combat"],["Hypogeum","The underground area beneath the arena"],["Velarium","A canvas awning that shaded spectators"]],
f:["The Colosseum had 80 entrances.","Its opening games reportedly lasted 100 days.","Its image appears on Italy’s five-cent euro coin."],
fun:"Some historians believe the arena may have been flooded for mock sea battles in its early years.",
tl:"Ang Colosseum ay malaking sinaunang arena sa gitna ng Roma, Italya. Libu-libong tao ang nanonood dito ng labanan ng mga gladiator at pangangaso ng hayop, at isa ito ngayon sa pinakatanyag na gusali sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"baseball",t:"Baseball",c:"How We Live",i:"⚾",w:"Baseball",r:["cricket","olymp","soccer","bball","japancountry"],
p:[["Bat and ball","Baseball is a bat-and-ball game between two teams of nine players."],["Run the bases","Batters hit the ball and run around four bases to score runs."],["Popular worldwide","It is especially popular in the Americas, Japan, South Korea and Taiwan."]],
l:"Baseball is a bat-and-ball sport played between two teams of nine players who take turns batting and fielding. The batting team tries to score runs by hitting a ball thrown by the opposing pitcher and running around four bases arranged in a diamond, while the fielding team tries to get batters and runners out. A game normally lasts nine innings. Baseball developed in the United States in the 19th century from older English bat-and-ball games and became known as America’s ‘national pastime’. It is also hugely popular in Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, Cuba, the Dominican Republic, Venezuela, Mexico and Puerto Rico. Softball is a related game with a larger ball and a smaller field.",
s:[["How it is played","Each inning, both teams bat once. The pitcher throws the ball towards home plate, and the batter tries to hit it into the field. After hitting, the batter runs to first base and beyond. A run scores when a player touches all four bases. A team bats until three of its players are out; after nine innings, the team with more runs wins."],["Getting out","A batter is out after three strikes, which are pitches swung at and missed or pitches in the strike zone not swung at. Batters are also out if a fielder catches a hit ball before it touches the ground, or if fielders tag them or touch a base with the ball before they reach it. A hit over the outfield fence is a home run."],["Origins","Games with bats, balls and bases were played in England for centuries, including rounders. In the 1840s, clubs in New York wrote down rules close to modern baseball. The first professional league, the National League, was founded in 1876. Major League Baseball in the United States and Canada is now the top professional league."],["Around the world","Baseball reached Japan in the 1870s, and Japan’s professional league is one of the strongest in the world; its high school tournament at Koshien Stadium is a national event. The World Baseball Classic, first held in 2006, brings national teams together. Baseball has been an Olympic sport at several Games and returns in 2028."]],
k:[["Players per team","Nine on the field"],["Game length","Nine innings"],["Bases","Four, in a diamond"],["Top league","Major League Baseball"],["World tournament","World Baseball Classic, since 2006"]],
d:[["1845","New York’s Knickerbocker Club writes early rules"],["1876","The National League is founded"],["1870s","Baseball introduced to Japan"],["1947","Jackie Robinson breaks the color barrier in the major leagues"],["1992","Baseball becomes an official Olympic sport"],["2006","First World Baseball Classic"]],
s2:[["Jackie Robinson","For decades, Black players were barred from Major League Baseball and played in the Negro Leagues. On 15 April 1947, Jackie Robinson took the field for the Brooklyn Dodgers, becoming the first Black player in the modern major leagues. He faced terrible abuse but became a star and a hero of the civil rights movement. Every year on 15 April, all major league players wear his number, 42."],["Shohei Ohtani","Japanese player Shohei Ohtani became a global star in the 2020s as a rare two-way player, excelling as both a pitcher and a hitter at the highest level, something few had done since Babe Ruth a century earlier. He helped Japan win the 2023 World Baseball Classic."]],
sp:["The diamond","The four bases form a square, called the diamond, with sides of 90 feet (about 27.4 meters). The pitcher throws from a raised mound about 18.4 meters from home plate. Outfield sizes vary from stadium to stadium, unlike most sports."],
g:[["Inning","A period in which each team bats once"],["Strike","A pitch the batter misses or does not swing at in the strike zone"],["Home run","A hit that lets the batter circle all the bases"],["Pitcher","The player who throws the ball to the batter"]],
f:["A regulation baseball has 108 double stitches.","Japan has won the World Baseball Classic three times.","Every major league player wears number 42 on Jackie Robinson Day."],
fun:"Baseball is one of the few major sports with no game clock for most of its history; a pitch timer was added in Major League Baseball only in 2023.",
tl:"Ang baseball ay larong may bat at bola sa pagitan ng dalawang koponang may tig-siyam na manlalaro. Tumatakbo ang mga batter sa apat na base upang makapuntos, at sikat ito sa Amerika, Hapon, Korea at Taiwan."});

ADDART({id:"lunarnewyear",t:"Lunar New Year",c:"How We Live",i:"🧧",w:"Lunar New Year",r:["festivals","china","korea","mandarin","buddh"],
p:[["A new year by the moon","Lunar New Year marks the start of a year in the traditional lunisolar calendar."],["Family and luck","It is a time for family reunions, feasts, red decorations and wishes for good luck."],["Celebrated widely","It is celebrated in China, Korea, Vietnam and by communities around the world."]],
l:"Lunar New Year is the celebration of the beginning of a new year in the traditional lunisolar calendars of East and Southeast Asia. It falls on the second new moon after the winter solstice, between 21 January and 20 February. Known as the Spring Festival in China, Seollal in Korea and Tết in Vietnam, it is the most important holiday of the year for well over a billion people, and it is also celebrated in Singapore, Malaysia, Indonesia and by communities worldwide. Customs include family reunion dinners, cleaning the home, red decorations, gifts of money in red envelopes, firecrackers, and lion and dragon dances. Each year is linked to one of 12 zodiac animals.",
s:[["When it falls","Traditional East Asian calendars combine the cycles of the moon and the sun. New Year’s Day falls on the second new moon after the winter solstice, so its date in the Western calendar changes each year. In China, celebrations traditionally last 15 days, ending with the Lantern Festival on the first full moon."],["Customs and traditions","Before the new year, families clean their homes to sweep away bad luck. Doors are decorated with red banners carrying wishes for happiness and wealth. On New Year’s Eve, families gather for a reunion dinner. Children and unmarried relatives receive red envelopes containing money. Firecrackers and fireworks greet the new year."],["The zodiac animals","Each year is associated with one of 12 animals in a repeating cycle: Rat, Ox, Tiger, Rabbit, Dragon, Snake, Horse, Goat, Monkey, Rooster, Dog and Pig. People born in a year are said to share qualities of its animal. In Vietnam, the Cat replaces the Rabbit and the Water Buffalo the Ox."],["The world’s biggest journey","Before the Spring Festival, hundreds of millions of people in China travel home to be with their families, in a travel season called chunyun, often described as the largest annual human migration on Earth. In 2023, the United Nations made Lunar New Year an official UN holiday."]],
k:[["Also called","Spring Festival, Seollal, Tết"],["Date","Between 21 January and 20 February"],["Lasts","Traditionally 15 days in China"],["Zodiac","12 animals in a cycle"],["2026","Year of the Horse"]],
d:[["c.2000 BC","Traditional origins of new year celebrations in China"],["1912","China adopts the Western calendar; the festival is renamed Spring Festival"],["1989","South Korea restores Seollal as a three-day holiday"],["2023","UN adds Lunar New Year to its holiday calendar"],["17 Feb 2026","The Year of the Horse begins"],["6 Feb 2027","The Year of the Goat begins"]],
s2:[["The legend of Nian","According to legend, a monster called Nian came every new year to eat crops, animals and even children. Villagers discovered that it feared the color red, loud noises and fire, so they hung red decorations and set off firecrackers to scare it away. These customs became part of the new year celebrations."],["Tết in Vietnam","In Vietnam, Tết Nguyên Đán is the biggest holiday of the year. Families decorate with peach blossoms in the north and yellow apricot blossoms in the south, make square sticky rice cakes called bánh chưng, and visit relatives. The first visitor to a home in the new year is believed to bring luck for the whole year."]],
sp:["Red envelopes","Red envelopes, called hongbao in Mandarin and lai see in Cantonese, are given by married adults to children and younger relatives. Red symbolizes good luck. Today, many people send digital red envelopes through mobile payment apps, with billions sent each new year."],
g:[["Lunisolar","A calendar based on both the moon and the sun"],["Hongbao","A red envelope containing money given as a gift"],["Zodiac","A cycle of animals linked to years"],["Chunyun","The Spring Festival travel season in China"]],
f:["Lunar New Year can fall on any date between 21 January and 20 February.","The Year of the Horse began on 17 February 2026.","In Vietnam’s zodiac, the Cat takes the place of the Rabbit."],
fun:"The number 8 is considered lucky because in Chinese it sounds like the word for wealth, so red envelopes often contain amounts with eights.",
tl:"Ang Lunar New Year ay pagdiriwang ng bagong taon ayon sa kalendaryong batay sa buwan at araw. Panahon ito ng pagsasama-sama ng pamilya, piging, pulang dekorasyon at pagbati ng suwerte sa Tsina, Korea, Vietnam at sa buong mundo."});

ADDART({id:"intramuros",t:"Intramuros",c:"Philippines",i:"🧱",w:"Intramuros",r:["manila","spanph","rizal","phrev","christ"],
p:[["The walled city","Intramuros is the historic walled city at the heart of Manila."],["Spanish Manila","It was the center of Spanish rule in the Philippines for over 300 years."],["Rebuilt after war","Destroyed in 1945, much of it has since been restored."]],
l:"Intramuros, Latin for ‘within the walls’, is the old walled city of Manila, on the south bank of the Pasig River near Manila Bay. It stands on the site of Maynila, a fortified settlement ruled by Rajah Sulayman before the Spanish arrived. After Miguel López de Legazpi made Manila the capital of the Spanish Philippines in 1571, the Spanish built a city of churches, convents, schools, government buildings and homes, protected from the late 16th century by stone walls about 4.5 kilometers long. For over three centuries it was the seat of colonial government and the Church. Almost entirely destroyed in the Battle of Manila in 1945, it has since been partly restored and is one of the country’s most visited historic sites.",
s:[["Building the walls","After the Chinese pirate Limahong attacked Manila in 1574, the Spanish began building stone defenses. Over the next two centuries, they constructed thick walls, bastions, gates and a moat around the city, which covered about 64 hectares. Fort Santiago guarded the mouth of the Pasig River."],["Life within the walls","Intramuros held the governor’s palace, the cathedral, many churches and convents, and the first universities in the Philippines, including the University of Santo Tomas, founded in 1611. For long periods, it was mainly home to Spanish residents and officials, while Filipinos and Chinese lived in districts outside the walls, such as Binondo."],["Fort Santiago","Fort Santiago, at the northwest corner of Intramuros, was a military headquarters and prison. José Rizal was held there before his execution on 30 December 1896, and a museum and brass footprints mark his final walk. During the Japanese occupation, many prisoners died in its dungeons."],["Destruction and restoration","In February 1945, during the battle to retake Manila from Japanese forces, Intramuros was almost completely destroyed, and many civilians were killed. Only San Agustin Church survived largely intact. Since 1979, the Intramuros Administration has restored walls, gates and buildings."]],
k:[["Meaning","‘Within the walls’ (Latin)"],["Founded","1571"],["Walls","About 4.5 km long"],["Area","About 64 hectares"],["Oldest stone church","San Agustin Church"]],
d:[["Before 1571","Rajah Sulayman’s fortified settlement of Maynila"],["1571","Legazpi establishes Spanish Manila"],["1574","Attack by Limahong leads to new defenses"],["1607","San Agustin Church is completed"],["1945","The Battle of Manila destroys most of the city"],["1979","The Intramuros Administration is created"]],
s2:[["San Agustin Church","San Agustin Church, completed in 1607, is the oldest stone church in the Philippines. It has survived earthquakes and the destruction of 1945. With three other Baroque churches in the country, it was listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1993. Its painted ceiling looks three-dimensional."],["Manila Cathedral","Manila Cathedral has been destroyed and rebuilt several times since the 1500s, by typhoons, fires, earthquakes and war. The present building was completed in 1958. It is the seat of the Archbishop of Manila."]],
sp:["Kalesas and bamboo bikes","Visitors to Intramuros can tour its streets by kalesa, a horse-drawn carriage, or on bicycles with bamboo frames made by a local social enterprise. Walking tours along the top of the walls give views of the city and Manila Bay."],
g:[["Intramuros","Latin for ‘within the walls’"],["Bastion","A part of a fortress wall that sticks out for defense"],["Moat","A deep ditch around a fortified place"],["Baroque","An ornate style of art and architecture"]],
f:["Intramuros covers about 64 hectares.","San Agustin Church is the oldest stone church in the Philippines.","Brass footprints in Fort Santiago mark José Rizal’s final walk."],
fun:"Several of Intramuros’ gates are still named after saints and Spanish officials, such as Puerta Real, the ‘Royal Gate’.",
tl:"Ang Intramuros, na ang ibig sabihin ay ‘sa loob ng pader’, ang makasaysayang lungsod na napapaderan sa puso ng Maynila. Ito ang sentro ng pamamahala ng Espanya nang mahigit 300 taon, at naibalik ang malaking bahagi nito matapos masira noong 1945."});

ADDART({id:"chocolatehills",t:"The Chocolate Hills",c:"Philippines",i:"⛰️",w:"Chocolate Hills",r:["bohol","caves","coral","tourism","conservation"],
p:[["Over a thousand hills","The Chocolate Hills are more than 1,200 rounded hills in Bohol."],["Turning brown","Their grass turns brown in the dry season, like chocolate drops."],["Made of limestone","They formed from ancient coral and shell deposits shaped by rain and erosion."]],
l:"The Chocolate Hills are a geological formation of at least 1,268 rounded, cone-shaped hills spread over more than 50 square kilometers in the province of Bohol in the central Philippines. Most are between 30 and 50 meters high, and the tallest reaches about 120 meters. Covered in grass, they turn chocolate brown during the dry season, giving them their name. The hills are made of limestone formed from ancient marine deposits, raised above the sea and then shaped by rainwater and erosion over millions of years. The Chocolate Hills were declared a National Geological Monument in 1988 and are one of the most famous sights in the Philippines.",
s:[["How they formed","Millions of years ago, the area was under a shallow sea, where corals, shells and other marine creatures built up layers of limestone. Movements of the Earth’s crust lifted these layers above sea level. Rainwater, which is slightly acidic, slowly dissolved and wore away the limestone, leaving the rounded hills. This type of landscape is called karst."],["The landscape","The hills are remarkably similar in shape, rising from flat farmland where rice and other crops are grown. They are covered in grass and few trees grow on them. A viewing deck on one of the hills in the town of Carmen offers a wide view across the hills."],["Legends","Local legends explain the hills in different ways. In one story, two giants fought for days, throwing rocks and sand at each other, then left the mess behind. In another, a giant named Arogo fell in love with a mortal woman, and when she died, his tears dried into the hills."],["Protecting the hills","The Chocolate Hills have been protected by law since the late 1990s. Quarrying and building on the hills are restricted. In 2024, photos of a resort built among the hills caused a national outcry and led to investigations and calls for stronger protection."]],
k:[["Province","Bohol"],["Number","At least 1,268"],["Typical height","30–50 m"],["Made of","Limestone"],["Protected","National Geological Monument, 1988"]],
d:[["Millions of years ago","Limestone forms under a shallow sea"],["Later","The land is raised and eroded into hills"],["1988","Declared a National Geological Monument"],["1997","Proclaimed a protected area"],["2013","A strong earthquake shakes Bohol"],["2024","Public outcry over a resort among the hills"]],
s2:[["The 2013 earthquake","On 15 October 2013, a magnitude 7.2 earthquake struck Bohol, killing over 200 people and damaging historic churches. Some of the Chocolate Hills were scarred by landslides, and the viewing deck was damaged, but the hills themselves remained."],["Tarsiers nearby","The forests of Bohol, not far from the Chocolate Hills, are home to the Philippine tarsier, one of the world’s smallest primates, with enormous eyes. Visitors often see both on the same trip, at protected sanctuaries."]],
sp:["Karst landscapes","Karst landscapes form where rainwater dissolves soluble rocks such as limestone, creating caves, sinkholes, towers and rounded hills. Other famous karst landscapes include Ha Long Bay in Vietnam and the tower hills of Guilin in China."],
g:[["Limestone","Rock formed mostly from the remains of marine creatures"],["Karst","A landscape shaped by the dissolving of soluble rock"],["Erosion","The wearing away of land by water, wind or ice"],["Geological monument","A protected natural feature of special scientific value"]],
f:["There are at least 1,268 Chocolate Hills.","The hills turn brown during the dry season.","The Chocolate Hills appear on the provincial flag and seal of Bohol."],
fun:"The hills look green for much of the year; they only look like chocolate in the dry season.",
tl:"Ang Chocolate Hills ay mahigit 1,200 bilugang burol sa Bohol. Nagiging kulay-tsokolate ang damo nito tuwing tag-araw, at nabuo ang mga ito mula sa sinaunang batong-apog na hinubog ng ulan at erosyon."});

ADDART({id:"undergroundriver",t:"The Puerto Princesa Underground River",c:"Philippines",i:"🦇",w:"Puerto Princesa Subterranean River National Park",r:["palawan","caves","conservation","tourism","bats"],
p:[["A river through a mountain","The Underground River flows for over 8 kilometers through a cave in Palawan."],["Straight to the sea","It is one of the world’s longest navigable underground rivers and flows directly into the sea."],["A protected wonder","It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the New Seven Wonders of Nature."]],
l:"The Puerto Princesa Underground River is a river that flows for about 8.2 kilometers through a limestone cave beneath the Saint Paul Mountain Range in Palawan, before emptying directly into the West Philippine Sea. It is one of the longest navigable underground rivers in the world, and its lower part is affected by the tides. Visitors explore the first stretch in paddle boats, seeing huge chambers, stalactites, stalagmites and rock formations, as well as bats and swiftlets. The river is the centerpiece of the Puerto Princesa Subterranean River National Park, which protects a full ‘mountain-to-sea’ ecosystem of forests, mangroves and coast. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1999.",
s:[["The cave","The river has carved a long cave through limestone over millions of years. Inside are vast chambers, including one called the Italian’s Chamber, about 360 meters long and among the largest cave chambers in the world. Dripping water has formed stalactites hanging from the ceiling and stalagmites rising from the floor."],["Life in the dark","The cave is home to several species of bats and swiftlets, which find their way in the darkness using echolocation. Fish, crabs, snakes and other creatures also live in or near the river. In 2010, scientists found fossils of a sea cow embedded in the cave rock, millions of years old."],["Mountain to sea","The national park protects forests that stretch from the summit of Mount Saint Paul down to the coast, with mangroves, beaches and coral. It is home to monkeys, monitor lizards, hornbills and many plants found only in Palawan. Indigenous Batak and Tagbanua communities live in the area."],["Visiting the river","Visitors travel by boat from the village of Sabang to the cave entrance, then ride in paddle boats with a guide and lamp for about 45 minutes. Visitor numbers are limited each day to protect the cave."]],
k:[["Location","Palawan"],["Underground length","About 8.2 km"],["Flows into","The West Philippine Sea"],["UNESCO site","Since 1999"],["New7Wonders of Nature","Named in 2012"]],
d:[["Millions of years","The river carves the cave through limestone"],["1971","Protected as a national park"],["1999","Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2011","Chosen in a worldwide vote as a wonder of nature"],["2010","Sea cow fossil found in the cave walls"],["2012","Officially named a New Seven Wonder of Nature"]],
s2:[["The Italian’s Chamber","One of the cave’s greatest spaces, the Italian’s Chamber, about 360 meters long, is named after Italian cave explorers who studied the cave. Its ceiling rises so high that it is hard to see with a lamp. Much of the cave beyond the tourist route can only be reached by experienced cavers."],["The New Seven Wonders of Nature","In a worldwide public vote, the Underground River was chosen in 2011 as one of the New Seven Wonders of Nature, officially confirmed in 2012, along with the Amazon, Ha Long Bay, Iguazu Falls, Jeju Island, Komodo and Table Mountain."]],
sp:["Echolocation","Bats and some swiftlets find their way in total darkness by making sounds and listening to the echoes that bounce back from walls and objects. This lets them fly through narrow cave passages without crashing."],
g:[["Stalactite","A rock formation hanging from a cave ceiling"],["Stalagmite","A rock formation rising from a cave floor"],["Navigable","Deep and wide enough for boats to travel on"],["Echolocation","Finding the way using echoes of sounds"]],
f:["The underground part of the river is about 8.2 kilometers long.","Its lower part rises and falls with the tides.","Visitor numbers are limited each day to protect the cave."],
fun:"Some cave formations have been given nicknames by guides, such as ‘the cathedral’ and shapes said to look like vegetables.",
tl:"Ang Puerto Princesa Underground River ay ilog na dumadaloy nang mahigit 8 kilometro sa loob ng kuweba sa Palawan at tuloy-tuloy sa dagat. Isa itong UNESCO World Heritage Site at isa sa New Seven Wonders of Nature."});

ADDART({id:"phchristmas",t:"Christmas in the Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"⭐",w:"Christmas in the Philippines",r:["christ","phfest","festivals","spanph","rice"],
p:[["The longest Christmas","Filipinos begin celebrating Christmas as early as September."],["Dawn masses","Simbang Gabi is a series of nine masses before Christmas Day."],["Lanterns and feasts","Star lanterns called parol and the Noche Buena feast are beloved traditions."]],
l:"Christmas in the Philippines is often called the longest Christmas season in the world, with decorations and carols appearing as early as September, at the start of the so-called ‘ber months’. The Philippines is one of the largest Christian countries in Asia, and Christmas is its most important holiday. Traditions blend Catholic customs introduced during Spanish rule with Filipino culture: the Simbang Gabi dawn masses, the star-shaped parol lanterns that light up homes and streets, carolers going from house to house, and the Noche Buena feast on Christmas Eve. The season continues into January, traditionally ending with the Feast of the Three Kings, or later with the Feast of the Santo Niño.",
s:[["Simbang Gabi","Simbang Gabi, ‘night mass’, is a series of nine masses held from 16 to 24 December, usually at dawn or in the evening. The tradition began during Spanish rule so that farmers could attend mass before working in the fields. Many believe that a person who completes all nine masses will have a wish granted."],["The parol","The parol is a star-shaped lantern representing the Star of Bethlehem that guided the Three Kings. Traditionally made of bamboo and paper, parols today also use capiz shells, plastic and colorful electric lights. San Fernando in Pampanga, known as the Christmas Capital of the Philippines, holds a Giant Lantern Festival with huge, dazzling lanterns."],["Noche Buena","After the last Simbang Gabi or midnight mass on Christmas Eve, families gather for Noche Buena, a feast that may include ham, queso de bola, spaghetti, lechon, fruit salad and rice cakes. Outside churches during the season, vendors sell bibingka and puto bumbong, warm rice cakes enjoyed after mass."],["Family and giving","Christmas is a time for family reunions, including overseas Filipinos returning home. Children visit godparents and relatives to receive gifts and money, a practice called pamamasko, and groups of carolers sing door to door. Many people also give to charity and share food with neighbors."]],
k:[["Season begins","As early as September"],["Simbang Gabi","16–24 December"],["Christmas Eve feast","Noche Buena"],["Symbol","The parol (star lantern)"],["Season ends","Early to mid-January"]],
d:[["September","The ‘ber months’ begin; decorations appear"],["16 Dec","Simbang Gabi begins"],["24 Dec","Last Simbang Gabi and Noche Buena"],["25 Dec","Christmas Day"],["1 Jan","New Year"],["Early Jan","Feast of the Three Kings"]],
s2:[["The Giant Lantern Festival","Every December, villages in San Fernando, Pampanga, compete in the Giant Lantern Festival with lanterns several meters across, lit by thousands of bulbs that flash in patterns to music. The tradition grew from smaller lanterns carried in religious processions."],["Christmas far from home","Millions of Filipinos work overseas. Many send home large boxes of gifts called balikbayan boxes before Christmas, and airports are crowded with returning workers. Filipino communities abroad hold their own Simbang Gabi masses."]],
sp:["Puto bumbong and bibingka","Puto bumbong is purple sticky rice steamed in bamboo tubes and served with butter, sugar and grated coconut. Bibingka is a rice cake baked in clay pots lined with banana leaves, with hot coals above and below. Both are classic treats sold outside churches during Simbang Gabi."],
g:[["Simbang Gabi","Nine masses held before Christmas"],["Parol","A star-shaped Christmas lantern"],["Noche Buena","The Christmas Eve feast"],["Pamamasko","Visiting relatives and godparents for Christmas gifts"]],
f:["Christmas songs can be heard in the Philippines as early as September.","Simbang Gabi lasts nine days.","San Fernando, Pampanga, is called the Christmas Capital of the Philippines."],
fun:"Some Filipinos say the season starts on 1 September because it is the first of the ‘ber months’: September, October, November and December.",
tl:"Ang Pasko sa Pilipinas ay sinasabing pinakamahabang Pasko sa mundo, na nagsisimula pa sa Setyembre. Kabilang sa mga tradisyon ang Simbang Gabi, ang parol at ang Noche Buena."});
